PRM80 control software
======================

This software is for use with the F4FEZ/F8EGQ PRM80 firmware.
Tested with PRM80xx Firmware V5 only (will have issues with earlier versions).

Since version 2.0 the program runs on Windows and Linux (and should run on macOS):

  - Build system: Maven (pom.xml). NetBeans, IntelliJ IDEA and VS Code open
    the project directly; the libraries are downloaded automatically.
  - Serial port: jSerialComm (replaces RXTX, no separate native libraries)
  - Look and feel: FlatLaf (start with --dark for the dark variant)
  - The old Swing Application Framework, swing-layout and swingworker
    libraries are no longer needed.

Requirements
------------
  - JDK 21 or newer (e.g. Eclipse Temurin, https://adoptium.net)
  - for development: Apache NetBeans (GUI builder for the .form files)

Build and run
-------------
  NetBeans:      File > Open Project > select this folder (control_software/trunk),
                 then "Clean and Build" and "Run".
  Command line:  mvn package
                 java -jar target/prm80-control-software-2.0.0.jar

The jar in target/ contains all libraries and runs on every computer with Java 21+.

Stand-alone program (no Java installation needed)
--------------------------------------------------
After building, run

  Windows:  package-windows.bat        -> target\dist\PRM80\PRM80.exe
  Linux:    ./package-linux.sh         -> target/dist/PRM80/bin/PRM80

The PRM80 folder contains its own Java runtime and can be copied or zipped.
"package-windows.bat msi" (needs WiX Toolset) or "./package-linux.sh deb"
build an installer / package instead.

Serial port on Linux
--------------------
The user needs access to the serial ports, usually by membership in the
group "dialout":   sudo usermod -aG dialout $USER   (then log in again)
Ports are named /dev/ttyUSB0, /dev/ttyACM0, /dev/ttyS0, ... on Linux and
COM1, COM2, ... on Windows. Select the port at the top of the main window.

Connecting
----------
Select the serial port in the main window and press "Connect". The list of
ports is updated each time it is opened (e.g. after plugging in a USB adapter).
All communication with the PRM80 runs in the background: the display changes
at once, "BUSY" is shown in the display until the PRM80 has answered.
The state of the PRM80 is read every 1.5 s (changes made at the radio).

Channel manager
---------------
Tools > Channel manager. It shows the channel file used last time.
  Read from PRM80 / Write to PRM80   exchange the channels with the radio
  Read / Save / Save As              channel file (CSV, separator ";", UTF-8)
The PRM80 has no memory for comments: they are only kept in the file. When the
channels are read from the PRM80, the comments of the list shown before are
kept for channels with the same number and frequency.

VFO mode
--------
In VFO mode "Shift enabled", "Shift positive" and "Shift Frequency" set the TX
frequency at once (TX = RX +/- shift). "Write to MEM" stores the VFO setting in
a memory channel: the channels of the PRM80 are shown, choose one or a new one.
In memory mode the three fields show the shift of the current channel.

Expert mode
-----------
Tools > Expert mode shows the functions for experts (restart, RAM/EEPROM,
serial monitor, A51 export, TCP server connection, virtual PRM for tests).

Zoom
----
The window can be resized with the mouse, the whole user interface zooms
with it. Alternatively: View menu, Ctrl + / Ctrl - / Ctrl 0 (100 %).
The zoom factor is shown in the status bar and remembered for the next start.

Command line options
--------------------
  -h,   --help           Print this help message
  -s,   --server=PORT    Server mode. Listen on TCP port PORT
  -p,   --port=SERIAL    Serial port for server mode (e.g. COM3 or /dev/ttyUSB0),
                         default: the port selected in the GUI options
  -d,   --debug          Set debug mode
        --dark           Use the dark look and feel

History
-------
